If you get the horn of plenty, the lack of food is hardly an issue.

> AC BONUS:
> 
> Barbarians are at their best when rushing boldly into combat without
> concern for bodily harm. Armor can only slow them down and suggests an
> unflattering meekness.
> 
> Barbarians have a base AC of 12 instead of 10, but get a +1 AC bonus
> for every armor slot(shirt, body armor, cloak, hat, boots, gloves,
> shield) they AREN'T wearing. This means that they start with a naked
> AC of 5 but gain little or no additional benefit from most of the
> common early armor.
> 
> ---
> 
> RECKLESSNESS BONUS:
> 
> These following benefits are only effective while wearing two or fewer
> pieces of armor in any of the seven slots. Upon putting on a third
> piece of armor, the barbarian gets a "Wearing so much armor makes you
> feel less dangerous message".
> 
> XP1: Magic Cancellation 1
> XP4: Magic Cancellation 2
> XP8: Magic Cancellation 3
> XP12: Magic Resistance
> 
> Also, an additional +1 AC bonus and +1 Damage bonus at every third
> level (3, 6, 9, etc.)

The reason that not wearing armour could be considered to be reckless is 
that it makes the situation much more dangerous. By giving the character 
all that AC and MR and MC3, how can not wearing armour still be 
considered to be reckless? Given 9 points of protection, +2 SDSM and a 
+1 [oDisplacement, a level 15 barbarian would be AC -15 (including the 
above bonus for not wearing 5 pieces of armour), MC3, MR, reflection, 
displacement, no spellcasting penalty from armour, fast, poison res, 
stealthy (the last three class abilities). What's so reckless about that?


